  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5f1214fb30866644fb811b04fa59a1f2, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::InfoOpener
  interactRange: 1.5
  popupController: {fileID: 7154850324694818045}
  titleText: {fileID: 568867888}
  infoText: {fileID: 1737973553}
  titleContent: Freedom & Emancipation
  infoContent: The road on the left represents the long march toward freedom, a journey
    that required a civil war to even begin. Frederick Douglass, pictured in the
    centre, was born into slavery, taught himself to read in secret, escaped, and
    became one of the most powerful voices in American history. He said power concedes
    nothing without a demand, and that remained true throughout this entire era.
    The painting on the right shows enslaved people waiting for midnight on December
    31, 1862, the moment the Emancipation Proclamation would take effect and declare
    them legally free. For millions of people, that was the longest night of their
    lives.
