  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5f1214fb30866644fb811b04fa59a1f2, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::InfoOpener
  interactRange: 1.5
  popupController: {fileID: 7154850324694818045}
  titleText: {fileID: 568867888}
  infoText: {fileID: 1737973553}
  titleContent: Underground Railroad
  infoContent: The Underground Railroad was not a real railroad. It was a secret
    network of safe houses and people willing to risk everything to help enslaved
    people escape North. The paintings here show the dangerous nighttime journeys
    made on foot and horseback through forests and fields. Harriet Tubman, pictured
    on the right, escaped slavery in 1849 and returned south thirteen more times
    to lead others out. She led over 70 people to freedom and never lost a single
    passenger. The lantern beside this plaque was the signal used along the route,
    a light in the dark meaning the path ahead was safe.
