  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5f1214fb30866644fb811b04fa59a1f2, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::InfoOpener
  interactRange: 1.5
  popupController: {fileID: 7154850324694818045}
  titleText: {fileID: 568867888}
  infoText: {fileID: 1737973553}
  titleContent: Athletes
  infoContent: Jackie Robinson broke Major League Baseball's colour barrier in 1947,
    enduring hostility from crowds and teammates to open the door for every Black
    athlete who followed. Muhammad Ali became heavyweight champion of the world and
    used his platform to speak on race and politics at enormous personal cost, refusing
    military service on moral grounds and having his title stripped as a result.
    Jesse Owens won four gold medals at the 1936 Berlin Olympics in front of Adolf
    Hitler, directly disproving Nazi theories of racial superiority on the world's
    biggest stage. The boxing gloves in the case represent the sport that produced
    some of the most iconic Black athletes in history, men who fought inside the
    ring and outside it.
