--- !u!114 &1664847814
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1664847813}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 98fc91ce19c38ab4d804cead25022986, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::MicrophoneInput.MuseumMicrophone
  micInputSystemA: {fileID: 1909268140}
  mouthObjectA: {fileID: 1909268139}
  subtitleTextTarget: {fileID: 1623590945}
  audiblePlayback: 1
  useSpatialAudio: 0
  spatialVoiceVolume: 1
  spatialVoiceMinDistance: 5
  spatialVoiceMaxDistance: 40
  introWalkTarget: {fileID: 1047829636}
  introVoiceLines:
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: be36e7b5ced854b4aa65563d3a826792, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 97d302562d923f3449b9cf0972a7af77, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 1d1da2d88d71f29499ca10603dd41da1, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 09337e6296707274f95a1add72090920, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 1052940d0beff6649b3810e63d988ec6, type: 3}
  introSubtitles:
  - Welcome to the Black History Museum.
  - This is not just a collection of facts and dates.
  - It is a story.
  - And it starts further back than most people realise.
  - Follow me.
  introTransitionDelays:
  - 0.5
  - 0.5
  - 0.5
  - 0.6
  - 0.4
  acts:
  - actName: Africa Before
    forcefield: {fileID: 0}
    actStartDelay: 0
    subDisplays:
    - displayName: Architecture & Cities
      spot: {fileID: 1366732143}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 0}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: df075491b347f554586b9586490395e4, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 473599ae4bf1341439c4f827111d8b7a, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 299dfabba1738f54083d9d2cd405392f, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: feac48a7572345247a390b570ee41fe8, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 7ff72fc101dd41e478b025352b3b4021, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - Great Zimbabwe was a stone city housing 18,000 people, built without a single
        drop of mortar.
      - The Great Mosque of Djenne has been standing for over 700 years.
      - Timbuktu had scholars traveling from Europe and Asia just to study there.
      - They were not coming to teach. They were coming to learn.
      - These cities were built, thriving, and trading long before European contact.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.4
      - 0.5
    - displayName: Arts & Culture
      spot: {fileID: 1308129408}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 1976917643}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: f80094758dcf3ea4f9aca4ef33860749, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 329bc86ebef4f5f44bd9ef3e7c81c683, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: d38a1d9e1eb9d614bbf1445ea1b9ebb3, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 132412e431f61ba45b9f84dd1ffbd961, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: e2936283602b09449bf7a829d74f9aea, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: f7e561215ee93144fa13205b6546d050, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - Every object in this room was made with a specific purpose.
      - The masks, the sculptures, the jewellery, none of it was decoration.
      - When a mask was worn, the person inside it was understood to become the spirit
        it represented.
      - These portrait heads come from the Yoruba city of Ife in Nigeria, dating
        back to the 12th century.
      - When Europeans encountered them in the 1800s, many refused to believe Africans
        had made them.
      - The craftsmanship speaks for itself.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.6
      - 0.6
      - 0.5
      - 0.4
    - displayName: The Great Empires
      spot: {fileID: 239199143}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 0}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 4b11ec27123e5e64985b0a4091d35478, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 295b61df20d89444fa8e49adc23190cb, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: c07cb990f88263549b4cb1ddd5b57b99, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 0097f6c02f0817841b89771fff68b31a, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 2820dfc4a575e3e498479e1c3c2d98bb, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 00127155980d4db4c9ae582820cf7ed2, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 54ab77a3fc1212f478607148a69bae79, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - Mansa Musa was so wealthy that when he passed through Egypt in 1324,
      - he gave away enough gold to crash their entire economy.
      - Just passing through.
      - The Kingdom of Kush had more pyramids than Egypt.
      - Timbuktu had one of the first universities on earth with over 25,000 students.
      - The Mali Empire, the Songhai, the Kingdom of Benin.
      - These were some of the most powerful civilisations the world has ever seen.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.3
      - 0.5
      - 0.6
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
    hasQuiz: 1
    quiz:
      questionText: Who was the richest person in history?
      option1: Mansa Musa
      option2: Julius Caesar
      option3: Shaka Zulu
      correctOptionIndex: 0
      quizIntroClip: {fileID: 0}
      quizIntroSubtitle: 
      correctClip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: fad135804118b924cad99fa802305200, type: 3}
      correctSubtitle: Correct! Mansa Musa of the Mali Empire is considered the wealthiest
        person in history.
      wrongClip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 7cd31d694b3ca864ab1372d16148f425, type: 3}
      wrongSubtitle: Not quite. Mansa Musa of the Mali Empire is considered the wealthiest
        person in history.
  - actName: Enslavement & Resistance
    forcefield: {fileID: 0}
    actStartDelay: 0
    subDisplays:
    - displayName: The Slave Trade
      spot: {fileID: 1498624688}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 1750087548}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 60155e7a458aca64399a44506b0425e6, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 3ca5be1f94f67fb438a594be3a1d89a5, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 2c5258e7bd28e7c439f92274130ba4de, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: f1bb158b85be02a4f8b185fe0306783b, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 7cacbc818538b7a43a32ba1decbb2dde, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: d0c490515e7865b488173c14fc1da232, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - Between the 1600s and 1800s, between 12 and 15 million people were taken
        from Africa by force.
      - Loaded onto ships, chained below deck, shipped across an ocean.
      - An estimated 2 million did not survive the crossing.
      - Those who arrived were sold, separated from their families, stripped of their
        names.
      - This was not a small event in history.
      - It reshaped the entire continent of Africa and the lives of every person
        on it.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.6
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.4
      - 0.5
    - displayName: Plantation Life
      spot: {fileID: 308399713}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 1521068400}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 4ad1cb5790ca6224285c2e8bf8c77960, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 18ce4241a9ec6464785ee4f2a270e951, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: b5702650177d8ae40bdc88fc6c3f0fe8, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: ad244b6d5d51d5d4782d84481ddcc66e, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 06e4e551c0e18814293c24cf8b2a8dcf, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: f7ebbcc1880192c428b1427f1742323f, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - For those who survived the crossing, this was daily life.
      - Working from before sunrise to after dark with nothing in return.
      - Families could be separated and sold on any given morning.
      - Despite that, communities held together.
      - They kept their music, their languages, their stories.
      - Entire cultures were maintained inside conditions designed to destroy them.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.4
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
    - displayName: The Underground Railroad
      spot: {fileID: 750647522}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 370785039}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: fd69143b2822e7c4599d50e1f051c238, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 2b78bd869d3acbf4f882894cd3514f95, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: de1e138d816976e478e3be542e70f425, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 27c1601d81a98ee459956584bd48187e, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: d734cd003a9819646ac1f17238b3cf45, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 2728c1f90a93e704786e5b48ded721a9, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: c288528925528aa46b9ca7558aaae99a, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - It took a civil war to end slavery in America.
      - Over 180,000 Black men fought in that war for a country that still legally
        owned them.
      - Lincoln signed the Emancipation Proclamation in 1863.
      - But freedom on paper and freedom in reality are two different things.
      - Jim Crow laws enforced segregation for another hundred years after that.
      - The fight for real equality was only just beginning.
      - And it would take another century of resistance to make it real.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.5
      - 0.6
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
    - displayName: Freedom & Emancipation
      spot: {fileID: 809303869}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 1538835464}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 026109ecc0ebd8b40a594f95e3f6c07c, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: e2c39000600233442a79041283aead52, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: bdc94cd7eb8a5b44899129a0f4de78f1, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 8129e9c2ea5ce0146a4f9c1f551bde9c, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 724d6014bf4751d488516455dc365ad0, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: b9d2d6ad428f46e46a2d7be7ed9a2210, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 2f5620511457f444c947ab95931aa6f1, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - Harriet Tubman escaped slavery in 1849.
      - Then she went back. Thirteen times.
      - Every trip south could have been her last.
      - She carried a pistol and had one rule. Nobody turns back.
      - She led over 70 people to freedom and never lost a single one.
      - She later led a military raid that freed over 700 more people in a single
        night.
      - A lit lantern meant the path ahead was safe. Follow it.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.6
      - 0.5
    hasQuiz: 1
    quiz:
      questionText: 'True or False: Harriet Tubman never lost a passenger.'
      option1: True
      option2: False
      option3: N/A
      correctOptionIndex: 0
      quizIntroClip: {fileID: 0}
      quizIntroSubtitle: 
      correctClip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: f661426da6699aa46845b2896c3ae461, type: 3}
      correctSubtitle: Correct! Harriet Tubman completed 13 missions and never lost
        a single passenger.
      wrongClip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 700092b1489de8045857252d480da205, type: 3}
      wrongSubtitle: Actually its true. Harriet Tubman completed 13 missions and
        never lost a single passenger.
  - actName: Black Excellence
    forcefield: {fileID: 0}
    actStartDelay: 0
    subDisplays:
    - displayName: The Harlem Renaissance
      spot: {fileID: 1715845013}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 1878763040}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: be00792ff00927a40818901b8f925c79, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 0fa4d3772e632b0409fd4de5881d2271, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: e29043a63923fa74fba453ac2ae642c5, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 921b7fe912585e34db67de11160d797c, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 75377b1549dde064586f8b8cdd835d0b, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: ffe6607988e36b04783a9cf12232d509, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - After the war, millions of Black Americans moved North.
      - In Harlem in the 1920s and 30s, something remarkable happened.
      - Langston Hughes. Zora Neale Hurston. Aaron Douglas. Billie Holiday. Louis
        Armstrong.
      - All producing some of the greatest work in American history at the same time.
      - The writing, the art, and the music that came out of Harlem changed American
        culture permanently.
      - And it came from people who had been carrying that creativity through everything.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.6
      - 0.5
    - displayName: Athletes
      spot: {fileID: 2099800392}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 404393183}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 49d34a19074a39e4cb8fea5886b41fae, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 63ff777f9de84b947b9f53f2e8c4f286, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 1b714067287ada14baafacf5dbc4d4e9, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 7875260b8a82be94e918b69b5b3d2a69, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 49d5d82519e76834e859ea1bd86a961c, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 3ad88b4b501a0dd479aa58ba37c19655, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: c352c5e15af3ebb4cb39561f43b47886, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - Jesse Owens won four gold medals at the 1936 Berlin Olympics.
      - Jackie Robinson entered Major League Baseball in 1947 knowing exactly the
        hostility waiting for him.
      - He went anyway and changed the sport forever.
      - Muhammad Ali won the heavyweight championship of the world.
      - Then gave up his title rather than fight in a war he did not believe in.
      - He lost three years of his career for that decision and did not back down.
      - These athletes were not just competing. Every win was a statement.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.5
      - 0.6
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
    - displayName: Inventors & Innovators
      spot: {fileID: 1644145735}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 338376951}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: fca73d46c3efa3e41a55299968bbea14, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 306438f80e862354bbe2790dbacf4bf8, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 537d28c4ce6e96d4793430a3c8e108f7, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: e0b66e94a476807458d888148363e427, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: de173f61a1869794984127ad55e398fe, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 38fb86e5834bbbe439ba9e9cf6f469dd, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: f89b24e7c4c0efe4eb7daf8522b77728, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - Garrett Morgan invented the traffic light in 1923.
      - The same man invented the gas mask that saved soldiers in World War One.
      - Lewis Latimer developed the carbon filament that made Edison's lightbulb
        last long enough to actually be useful.
      - Madam C.J. Walker built a haircare empire from nothing and became the first
        self-made female millionaire in American history.
      - Mark Dean co-created the IBM personal computer and holds three of the nine
        original patents.
      - Mae Jemison became the first Black woman in space in 1992.
      - All of this achieved while navigating a system built to exclude them.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.6
      - 0.6
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
    - displayName: Leaders & Trailblazers
      spot: {fileID: 1337140124}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 938504432}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: e6aec9ca39676ff4ebe4ee8e9db92926, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 4eed22bc72ca4a741aaf27ff540a70fc, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: fa2bb64c550a07d4e993434ef63ba89e, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: e52150ff9a96f4f47a95c7b8299166a2, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 9fa87e7140498d744a81056933651e15, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: e877db0ef7ceec04d8be7fea36b89840, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 873eadc6d90c584479dedcafa3c4bff1, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 10270de7e9ee3744398e0b7c487e345a, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - Nelson Mandela was imprisoned for 27 years.
      - He was offered release if he publicly renounced his movement. He refused.
      - He walked out in 1990 and led South Africa out of apartheid.
      - Rosa Parks refused to give up her seat in 1955 and sparked a 381 day boycott.
      - Frederick Douglass taught himself to read in secret as an enslaved person
      - and became one of the most important voices in American history.
      - George Floyd was killed in May 2020.
      - Within weeks, people in over 60 countries were in the streets.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.3
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
    - displayName: The Modern Era
      spot: {fileID: 1586020290}
      defaultLookAt: {fileID: 967432190}
      voiceLines:
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 29337b33fb0c52b4fa8a54c11485f2bc, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: c5ab156f58488f747bb3df5f1a0e74f0, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 3f734ed53ce8c4948b3f8cdcd40cfcbc, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 3bd4e56795e08964da8af5b856f6136d, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 06cec63e06205f14d9fbd1e9de3cf7c5, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 9fd8ff451fedefe44ab55af73001631b, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: c1197f506898db9419d8778c1c932382, type: 3}
      - {fileID: 8300000, guid: a68234427bc11c944b05d165e73c9bce, type: 3}
      subtitles:
      - In 2008 Barack Obama became the 44th President of the United States.
      - In 2022 Ketanji Brown Jackson became the first Black woman on the Supreme
        Court.
      - Simone Biles is the most decorated gymnast in history.
      - Kendrick Lamar won the Pulitzer Prize, the first rapper to ever receive it.
      - Serena Williams won 23 Grand Slam titles.
      - None of this was given.
      - Every person in this room built their achievement inside systems that were
        not designed for them.
      - And that thread runs all the way back to the first room you walked into.
      transitionDelays:
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.5
      - 0.4
      - 0.6
      - 0.5
    hasQuiz: 0
    quiz:
      questionText: 'True or False: Kendrick Lamar was the first rapper to win a
        Pulitzer Prize.'
      option1: True
      option2: False
      option3: N/A
      correctOptionIndex: 0
      quizIntroClip: {fileID: 0}
      quizIntroSubtitle: 
      correctClip: {fileID: 0}
      correctSubtitle: Correct! Kendrick Lamar won the Pulitzer Prize for Music in
        2018.
      wrongClip: {fileID: 0}
      wrongSubtitle: Actually true. Kendrick Lamar won the Pulitzer Prize for Music
        in 2018.
  clearSubtitleWhenSequenceEnds: 1
